<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Latest news from the Middle East, daily international news reports - France 24</title><subtitle type="html">FRANCE 24’s coverage and analyses of political and social issues across the Middle East, including Egypt, the Gulf states, Iraq, Israel, Jordan, Lebanon, Saudi Arabia, Syria, Yemen and beyond.</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/rss</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:20:07Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Israelis await final election results as difficult choices lie ahead</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b9140d94e3f4f095c59e2ee362fe5743470be037c35fc7c987404d04603ac950</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:20:07Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T12:59:18Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-israelis-await-final-election-results-as-difficult-choices-lie-ahead--611940c40736.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-israelis-await-final-election-results-as-difficult-choices-lie-ahead" /><summary type="html">Israelis awaited the final election results on Thursday to confirm what appeared to be taking shape: neither Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and his allies nor the array of parties seeking to oust him seemed likely to secure a majority of seats in parliament. FRANCE 24's Clovis Casali reports from Jerusalem.</summary></entry><entry><title>Live: Qatar denies interference in Israeli elections, calls accusations 'dangerous'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:189dca292542528412d33e13f72cca96947934a803334ea53a8b734d856b8a96</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T03:36:46Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261029-live-israeli-elections-are-coming-to-a-close-with-no-majority-to-be-seen--43db87e7143d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261029-live-israeli-elections-are-coming-to-a-close-with-no-majority-to-be-seen" /><summary type="html">Qatar on Thursday rejected accusations that it interfered in Israeli elections to influence the results, calling them "dangerous". Israel’s Central Elections Committee called on the Shin Bet chief to investigate allegations of foreign interference, as the final results remain pending. Follow our liveblog for the latest updates.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Historic gains for Arab-majority parties in Israeli election</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b889c621bbf0a7d0861654584d9b1b8b19dfc8cc97f9831e3b064dd9f94ab8f0</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T20:32:29Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/historic-gains-for-arab-majority-parties-in-israeli-election--ef69df2f3ac1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/historic-gains-for-arab-majority-parties-in-israeli-election" /><summary type="html">Two Arab-majority parties are set to win a historic 19 seats in total, amid a record turnout at the polls in Israel on Tuesday. Voters from the community, descendants of Palestinians who stayed after Israel's creation and who make up around 20 percent of the population, have been enraged by the Netanyahu government's ultra-nationalist Jewish vision for the country and the Palestinian territories. FRANCE 24's Emerald Maxwell reports.</summary></entry><entry><title>Which way will Israel break? Netanyahu still in it after election night surprises</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1c27ae7a4ac03e6f9387d4b696d66793cacde9a53e3b93fd64a975964fa62559</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T19:34:30Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261028-which-way-will-israel-break-netanyahu-still-in-it-after-election-night-surprises--5418ddd2326d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261028-which-way-will-israel-break-netanyahu-still-in-it-after-election-night-surprises" /><summary type="html">In Israel, the surprises only start with election night. They're still counting ballots in a vote that featured the highest turnout in decades, historic inroads by Arab parties and Benjamin Netanyahu's worst result since 1999. And yet, Israel's longest-serving prime minister still has a path to staying in power. As we await the definitive makeup of the next parliament, our panel breaks out the calculators and pie charts: in what scenario does Netanyahu survive? Is the issue with the right-wing Likud leader or his policies?</summary><category term="The Debate" /></entry><entry><title>'Israeli electorate has moved rightward: Many underestimated extent of fracture in Israeli society'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:eb2e1422a763c735adf30eee48f0a5ab27917db4b07a5e9ffc684ab2126388c1</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T19:12:30Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/israeli-electorate-has-moved-rightward-many-underestimated-extent-of-fracture-in-israeli-society--c43137dced55.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israeli-electorate-has-moved-rightward-many-underestimated-extent-of-fracture-in-israeli-society" /><summary type="html">Erin Ogunkeye is pleased to welcome Dr. Shira Efron, RAND's Distinguished Israel Policy Chair. “Many of us, myself included, underestimated the extent of the fracture in Israeli society.” It reflects a political system in which ideological polarisation, the rightward shift of the Jewish electorate and the contested legitimacy of Arab political participation have sharply narrowed the possibilities for coalition-building. Dr. Efron illustrates a political landscape in which Netanyahu’s governing bloc has lost ground, yet still has “more cards, or better cards, to play,” she argues, whether through another election, a national unity government or attempts to draw opposition partners into his orbit.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel election: What impact on Iran as the war in the Middle East continues?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:da97f90a91f5d73d86bd5fbe4b89bda1fd3982b97e035ef79aca7bb9056cc11f</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T16:54:33Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israel-election-what-impact-on-iran-as-the-war-in-the-middle-east-continues--3eb40a582df2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israel-election-what-impact-on-iran-as-the-war-in-the-middle-east-continues" /><summary type="html">Benjamin Netanyahu is expected to face greater constraints as Israel’s caretaker prime minister following Tuesday’s election. However, experts say his government could retain significant powers, including the authority to wage war against Iran. So what impact will the election have on Israel’s approach to Iran? France 24’s International Affairs Editor Angela Diffley shares further insights.</summary></entry><entry><title>Arab parties ‘have woken up’: Israel's uncertain election may deliver surprise kingmakers</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:157bdc866ee39202db9ab956162f77a254402c97340c15d3267c23fd45537023</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T16:04:21Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261028-arab-parties-have-woken-up-israel-uncertain-election-may-deliver-surprise-kingmakers--f902aaf3d82e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261028-arab-parties-have-woken-up-israel-uncertain-election-may-deliver-surprise-kingmakers" /><summary type="html">Uncertainty hung over Israel’s election Wednesday as neither Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu’s right-wing, religious bloc nor the bloc formed by his centrist rival Gadi Eisenkot appeared to be heading for a majority in the Knesset. But there was a surprise: an unusually strong showing by Arab parties could make them unexpected kingmakers.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Israeli election: Lebanon expects little change in Israeli policy</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3c7765df972dfca41d0935d16116820f02443a0a46460d67b62ae148c257be3b</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T15:36:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israeli-election-lebanon-expects-little-change-in-israeli-policy--3b56afe2199d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israeli-election-lebanon-expects-little-change-in-israeli-policy" /><summary type="html">The war in the Middle East never really stopped, but Lebanese expect little change in Israeli policy after Israel's elections. In Israel, as votes are counted, no clear winner has emerged yet. France 24's correspondent Renée Davis reports the latest.</summary></entry><entry><title>West Bank: Settlers using drones to monitor Palestinians</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:dbf53ac906655f8dadbdb095503ca15a2850a0474af875c46725b137da2818f8</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T13:39:26Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20261028-west-bank-settlers-using-drones-to-monitor-palestinians--15ca13162004.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20261028-west-bank-settlers-using-drones-to-monitor-palestinians" /><summary type="html">In the occupied West Bank, Palestinian farmers are speaking out against the extensive use of drones by Israeli settlers and the Israeli military. According to activists and researchers, this practice is aimed, in part, at monitoring new Palestinian construction. In 2026, the Israeli government allocated a budget of 50 million shekels (over 14 million euros) to outposts and illegal settlements in the West Bank to fund so-called "security" equipment – which includes these drones.</summary><category term="The Observers" /></entry><entry><title>'No clear winner' from Israeli election, expert says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0f10ddcabb672ba96c203015cfc8f53759f48d9cd71f1dd2b31f05b30f762786</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T12:49:36Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261028-no-clear-winner-from-israeli-election-expert-says--0d17e93e168a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261028-no-clear-winner-from-israeli-election-expert-says" /><summary type="html">In this edition of Perspective, we focus on the results of the election in Israel. For analysis, we speak to Yossi Mekelberg, senior consulting fellow at Chatham House's Middle East and North Africa Programme. He tells us there is "no clear winner" from the country's pivotal vote.</summary><category term="Perspective" /></entry><entry><title>Israeli election: PM loses a significant number of seats since 2022 election</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:095186e434f70c5267f5d11371959a50135d48d6d5ea1afd90bdcadb5c3d16e6</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T11:56:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israeli-election-pm-loses-a-significant-number-of-seats-since-2022-election--1098b98779c3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israeli-election-pm-loses-a-significant-number-of-seats-since-2022-election" /><summary type="html">Israel was counting votes on Wednesday after pivotal elections in which exit polls showed no clear winner. Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu’s bloc appears to have lost a significant number of seats compared with the previous election in 2022. France 24’s International Affairs Editor Angela Diffley shares further insights.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israeli election: As votes are counted, no clear winner emerges</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6bbde6305333f1e2896e04fd322fb1ca64524a1678cfff2b6891f518a4222955</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T11:32:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israeli-election-as-votes-are-counted-no-clear-winner-emerges--a032d987794c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israeli-election-as-votes-are-counted-no-clear-winner-emerges" /><summary type="html">Anxious Israelis watched election results take shape Wednesday after the highest turnout in over a quarter-century, as no clear winner emerged and weeks of negotiating could lie ahead for Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and an array of opposition parties trying to unseat him. France 24 correspondent in Jerusalem Noga Tarnopolsky reports the latest.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel: Netanyahu edges closer to a majority</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1715f3cd3dd7d2ecf86b9f46e1bcc1597e21ccc28441c030121ad3827cde89a0</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T10:20:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/israel-netanyahu-edges-closer-to-a-majority--d7e6413659b3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israel-netanyahu-edges-closer-to-a-majority" /><summary type="html">The final results are expected tonight or tomorrow morning. For now, the picture is looking increasingly favourable for Benjamin Netanyahu and his bloc, but the Israeli prime minister is still short of a clear victory, as he needs 61 seats to secure a parliamentary majority. He addressed his supporters at the National Congress Centre at around 3 a.m. local time. France 24’s Clovis Casali joins us live from Jerusalem with the latest.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israeli election: 'Wishful thinking' for pro- and anti-Netanyahu parties?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:617f22099178167e3f808228240f937e3c7f3a3e77acfeec24d8ff9fa33d007b</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T10:15:25Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261028-israeli-election-wishful-thinking-for-pro-and-anti-netanyahu-parties--3443bd974576.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261028-israeli-election-wishful-thinking-for-pro-and-anti-netanyahu-parties" /><summary type="html">PRESS REVIEW – Wednesday, October 28: Papers react to the ongoing election results in Israel. Next: Macklemore's Free Palestine tour is coming to Paris. Also: antidepressants don't work the way we thought. Finally: a sneak peek into boomer divorcées' dating lives.</summary><category term="Press Review" /></entry><entry><title>Israelis await final election results as Netanyahu remains far from defeated</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fb43ace74e03d4d41ec4676681649de3f0e7e0c4cd7d661a0b2d3d5dd7a7c0a5</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T10:12:33Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israelis-await-final-election-results-as-netanyahu-remains-far-from-defeated--7f1ca5f482ca.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israelis-await-final-election-results-as-netanyahu-remains-far-from-defeated" /><summary type="html">Arab-led parties appear to have made a historic showing in the Israeli election, but it’s not clear if other opponents of Netanyahu's government would form a coalition with them for a majority in the next parliament. Issues around the Palestinians and their descendants remain incredibly sensitive in Israel.
 
France 24 24's International Affairs Commentator Douglas Herbert shares further insights.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel: The economic challenges ahead</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:330385126165203d91e7f0074154c151e62cd8b456a4846db60c119ccd7abc6d</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T09:43:01Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/israel-the-economic-challenges-ahead--3a58a6b976c6.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israel-the-economic-challenges-ahead" /><summary type="html">Questions over the future of Israel’s economy are looming in the wake of the elections. With one of the highest costs of living in the region, and defence spending continuing to rise, households are facing growing financial pressure. Caitlin Brown reports.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel election: There's no clear winner</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:009a7a4efeaeb422a889189d10088a8393ecc2df1834858f3f0aeb38ac80d5c8</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T09:40:15Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/israel-election-there-s-no-clear-winner--4b8ec9a18934.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israel-election-there-s-no-clear-winner" /><summary type="html">The path ahead remains uncertain in the Knesset in Jerusalem, as the Central Elections Committee continues to count the votes. With more than 60% of ballots tallied, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu’s camp is narrowly ahead of the opposition, but still short of a clear majority. 

These are still partial results and could change, meaning no clear winner has emerged so far. Both leading candidates for the prime minister’s office traded barbs in their respective speeches after polls closed. Antonia Kerrigan reports.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel: Netanyahu and Eisenkot both pledge to form government</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2158698a168dfa156147c54409e7360b14ad985c5451d315d4df3ce20cd4e939</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T09:06:44Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israel-netanyahu-and-eisenkot-both-pledge-to-form-government--c4056162f76f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israel-netanyahu-and-eisenkot-both-pledge-to-form-government" /><summary type="html">🗳️ Both Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin #Netanyahu and rival Gadi #Eisenkot pledged to form a government as the two are projected to finish neck and neck in #Israel's critical election Tuesday. 
 
Exit polls show both blocs falling short of a parliamentary majority.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel election: Netanyahu and Eisenkot battle to form government after deadlock</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ac54b78f78b13e29c48b9dd49f5ca1aa8b580bed2fbf983c70f408cfb6c44acd</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T22:58:53Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261027-israel-election-record-turnout-netanyahu-eisenkot-deadlock--3bd6be8cd195.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261027-israel-election-record-turnout-netanyahu-eisenkot-deadlock" /><summary type="html">Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and rival Gadi Eisenkot on Wednesday both pledged to form a government after an election left neither bloc with a parliamentary majority. Record voter turnout followed a divisive campaign dominated by the October 7, 2023 attack, while Arab Israeli parties made significant gains.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Israel: First exit polls show no clear winner, Arab parties make gains</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d94e27953da4992d3235d66854d938a45839d447a125250fc581b70198af2301</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T21:41:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-israel-first-exit-polls-show-no-clear-winner-arab-parties-make-gains--0bce30bad3ae.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-israel-first-exit-polls-show-no-clear-winner-arab-parties-make-gains" /><summary type="html">Exit polls on Tuesday showed that the opposition bloc led by Israeli opposition candidate Gadi Eisenkot gained a narrow edge over Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's Likud bloc in the Knesset. 
 
Likud, however, was projected to be the largest single party in the Israeli parliament.</summary></entry><entry><title>Bibi's moment of truth: Israel votes in first general election since October 7 attacks</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5ab7a4db92665974a10d24f4a06d7f74c62a00167995da2d3d9783180ce5ca8e</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T21:26:10Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261027-bibi-s-moment-of-truth-israel-votes-in-first-general-election-since-october-7--f604a5598a00.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261027-bibi-s-moment-of-truth-israel-votes-in-first-general-election-since-october-7" /><summary type="html">The turnout tells the tale. Israelis are casting ballots in their largest numbers in decades in the first general election since the October 7 attacks of 2023. The vote is seen as a referendum on the bloodiest day in the nation's history. But more broadly, what kind of Israel do citizens want?</summary><category term="The Debate" /></entry><entry><title>Netanyahu govt has 'attacked' Israeli judicial system, Supreme Court and democratic institutions</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:be6d8ebdde72ed04941927494eccfea02c81e7adb41a415a3511ede8676716da</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T19:44:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/netanyahu-govt-has-attacked-israeli-judicial-system-supreme-court-and-democratic-institutions--488e7af5b4c3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/netanyahu-govt-has-attacked-israeli-judicial-system-supreme-court-and-democratic-institutions" /><summary type="html">Annette Young is pleased to welcome Alain Greilsammer, Professor of Political Science at Bar-Ilan University in Tel Aviv. In the wake of the October 7 attacks, regional tensions with Iran, and deepening domestic divisions, Greilsammer argues that the election will be fundamentally shaped by concerns over democratic accountability, judicial independence and the unequal distribution of civic obligations. He warns Netanyahu’s policies risk transforming Israel into “something like an authoritarian or non-democratic state.” He suggests that Israel’s Arab political parties could play a decisive role in determining the election’s outcome.</summary></entry><entry><title>Ibrahim Abu Ahmad on Israeli election: 'Crucial for Arab citizens to have ally in Jewish community'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4efd09c696d93ffed15990b1d0a13b55ade0f527c8d1802d4e8c299659b2805d</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T18:59:43Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-ibrahim-abu-ahmad-on-israeli-election-crucial-for-arab-citizens-to-have-ally-in-jewish-comm--5d70dfe67cb8.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-ibrahim-abu-ahmad-on-israeli-election-crucial-for-arab-citizens-to-have-ally-in-jewish-community" /><summary type="html">Erin Ogunkeye is pleased to welcome Ibrahim Abu Ahmad, Author, Palestinian Israeli political and peace activist and Head of Social Media for Israel's left-wing Democrats. “Currently, the sentiment in Israel is built around fear and trauma,” explains Ahmad. His most striking observation concerns the apparent contradiction between political leadership and public sentiment: “Many want to see an Arab-Jewish coalition. It's the leadership that is afraid to say it.” Ultimately, he moves beyond conventional electoral calculations to frame Arab-Jewish partnership as a question of democratic survival.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israeli elections a 'referendum' on Netanyahu, security and whether Israel will 'remain a democracy'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1ea45c9ca1ca6d3811d64f50c4e046795f0e0e6d89e75d4e57c117ac530de881</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T18:34:19Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/israeli-elections-a-referendum-on-netanyahu-security-and-whether-israel-will-remain-a-democracy--60e9359e9e8a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israeli-elections-a-referendum-on-netanyahu-security-and-whether-israel-will-remain-a-democracy" /><summary type="html">Erin Ogunkeye is pleased to welcome Ron Gerlitz, CEO of aChord Center at Hebrew University, an Israeli organization specialising in the social psychology of intergroup relations. “The main issue in this election is the fight for Israeli democracy,” explains Gerlitz. And beneath the struggle over democratic institutions lies the erosion of Benjamin Netanyahu's longstanding association with national security. Gerlitz also examines the psychological foundations of Netanyahu's enduring support, the influence of conspiracy narratives, the electoral significance of Arab parties and the uncertain future of the two-state solution.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israelis vote in a tense election: What's at stake?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5a7da960dfae3dbcd0261edab7095c9099c2e3c5ef9a00044f13fa5fbbd37da7</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T16:59:06Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/israelis-vote-in-a-tense-election-what-s-at-stake--6f012b5ea955.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israelis-vote-in-a-tense-election-what-s-at-stake" /><summary type="html">Israelis were voting Tuesday (October 27) in a tense national election widely seen as a referendum on Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's leadership since the Hamas-led Oct. 7, 2023, attack on Israel and the wars that have since shaken the Middle East.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel elections: voter turnout reaches 55% by 4pm local time</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cc2a6259e774975586b0520f9604093da96a3d1fe633e2547aabda34f9f31e34</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T16:52:33Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/israel-elections-voter-turnout-reaches-55-by-4pm-local-time--cae49430dc2a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israel-elections-voter-turnout-reaches-55-by-4pm-local-time" /><summary type="html">Israelis flocked to the polls Tuesday (October 27) in turnout unseen in decades, with a deeply divided electorate delivering a verdict on veteran right-wing Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu in the first election since the October 7, 2023 attack. FRANCE24's Kethevane Gorjestani tells us more.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel votes in decisive election: What happens after polls close?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:63f3865b7f6e1d933b3fef42684839506ab76bf921b9c23fa5d6b83e5ef88a58</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T14:55:49Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-israel-s-unique-political-system-what-happens-after-polls-close--188424ee9721.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-israel-s-unique-political-system-what-happens-after-polls-close" /><summary type="html">Israelis are voting in a pivotal election Tuesday (October 27) as their country wrestles with unresolved wars, continues to reel from Hamas' 2023 attack and is deeply divided over the character of the state and the right person to lead it. Details by FRANCE 24's International Affairs Editor, Angela Diffley.</summary></entry><entry><title>Turnout high as Israeli voters deliver verdict on Netanyahu</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a1b4d0911609b434dade3f6bb626715dbcd00b96c0e95b5a6b98545a723d10f2</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T14:45:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/turnout-high-as-israeli-voters-deliver-verdict-on-netanyahu--bed5786a98df.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/turnout-high-as-israeli-voters-deliver-verdict-on-netanyahu" /><summary type="html">Israelis flocked to the polls Tuesday (October 27) in turnout unseen in decades, with a deeply divided electorate delivering a verdict on Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu in the first election since the October 7, 2023 attack. Former FRANCE 24 correspondent, Irris Makler, tells us more.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel: Netanyahu supporters gather as PM votes</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:54a985ea65dc771bef044288f801eedd660ada563e5f61dc6ca8dceded1c75f3</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T14:37:39Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/israel-netanyahu-supporters-gather-as-pm-votes--3f57cd1da13b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israel-netanyahu-supporters-gather-as-pm-votes" /><summary type="html">Supporters of Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu gathered outside a polling station where the leader was voting on Tuesday. This is Israel's first election since the October7, 2023 Hamas attack and is seen as a referendum on Netanyahu. FRANCE 24's Clovis Casali reports from Jerusalem.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israeli election: A referendum on 'Bibi' Netanyahu?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fb8cc31eb61c6f0d8830bffd0468b611cffd1dd3eb7f0d05727b001792cd0f6e</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:39Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T12:52:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261027-israeli-election-a-referendum-on-bibi-netanyahu--42870793125e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261027-israeli-election-a-referendum-on-bibi-netanyahu" /><summary type="html">PRESS REVIEW – Tuesday, October 27: The Israeli, Palestinian and Lebanese papers break down what's at stake on election day for Israel and its neighbours. Also, the French government announces its first major concessions to protesting high school students, hoping to calm the movement. Finally, Harry Kane's Ballon d'Or means Lamine Yamal and Kylian Mbappé will have to wait their turn.</summary><category term="Press Review" /></entry></feed>
